 ## Get set up
 
-1. Install with `/add-plugin pstack` in chat, or from Customize in the sidebar.
-2. Run [`/setup-pstack`](../setup-pstack/SKILL.md). It asks for a reasoning budget, maps a model to each role, and writes a rule. The rule applies to new chats. Then it offers once each to generate a verification skill and a formal verification skill for the project, unless the project already has one.
-3. Start a real task with `/poteto-mode`, a goal, and a check that can pass or fail.
+1. Install the Codex build of pstack. There is no `/add-plugin` or Customize sidebar in Codex. Clone `https://github.com/foxytanuki/pstack-opencodex`, run `python3 tools/pstack_opencodex.py build`, then `python3 tools/pstack_opencodex.py install --target ~/.agents/skills` for every project, or `--target <project>/.agents/skills` for one project. If the user already keeps pstack skills in another skills folder, such as `~/.codex/skills`, keep that folder as the target instead of guessing a path. The installer symlinks into the checkout's `dist/skills/`, so keep the checkout in place. Start a new Codex session afterward. Codex must run through opencodex with a model catalog.
+2. Run [`$setup-pstack`](../setup-pstack/SKILL.md). It asks for a reasoning budget, maps a model from the catalog to each role, and writes `~/.codex/pstack-models.md`. The settings apply to new sessions. `python3 tools/pstack_opencodex.py check-models` validates the file, and `check-runtime --parent-model <model>` is the read-only preflight before delegating to a routed child such as Claude or Grok. Then it offers once each to generate a verification skill and a formal verification skill for the project, unless the project already has one.
+3. Start a real task with `$poteto-mode`, a goal, and a check that can pass or fail.
 
